// `mutable_set_from_array` takes either world -- an array or a MutableArray -- which is what
// `either_world()` in the type checker means. It is a separate name rather than another source for
// `mutable`, because it **deduplicates**: [1, 2, 1, 3, 2] becomes three elements, and a reader of
// `mutable(xs)` would have no reason to expect that from a conversion.
//
// This fixture used to cover three more `from` constructors -- `mutable_array_from`,
// `mutable_map_from`, `mutable_set_from` -- and the clone each of them performed when handed a
// handle instead of a value. All three were removed once `mutable` covered the immutable-to-builder
// conversion they were mostly used for: keeping both would have put two spellings on one question,
// and the clone half had no caller anywhere outside this file. So there is now no way to clone a
// builder, deliberately rather than pending.
//
// What is still worth pinning is that the conversion's result is **independent of its source**: the
// last block writes 999 through the handle and the source Map still reads 1.
fn main() {
    val xs = mutable([3, 1, 2])
    push(xs, 4)
    print(snapshot(xs))

    // either world, and deduplicating in both
    val from_value = mutable_set_from_array([1, 2, 1, 3, 2])
    print(mutable_set_items(from_value))
    val from_handle = mutable_set_from_array(xs)
    print(mutable_set_items(from_handle))

    // the source value is untouched by writes through the handle
    val source = Map<string, int> { "k": 1 }
    val h = mutable(source)
    mutable_map_set(h, "k", 999)
    print(map_get(source, "k"))
    print(mutable_map_get(h, "k"))
}
